# Kotlin/Android patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Kotlin coroutines and Jetpack Compose design. Applies only to
`*.kt`/`*.kts` files.

## Coroutines: structured concurrency

- Launch a coroutine from a scope tied to the lifecycle that owns it —
  `viewModelScope` inside a `ViewModel`, `lifecycleScope` inside an
  `Activity`/`Fragment`, or a plain `coroutineScope { }`/`suspend`
  function for work with no lifecycle owner. Never `GlobalScope.launch`/
  `GlobalScope.async`: `GlobalScope` is unstructured — nothing cancels it
  when the screen or the ViewModel goes away, so it outlives the UI it was
  started for and can update state, navigate, or write to a destroyed
  view.
- When a function needs to run several suspend calls concurrently, wrap
  them in `coroutineScope { }` and use `async`/`await`, not a `launch`
  fired without an `await` point — `coroutineScope` waits for every child
  to finish (or cancels the rest if one fails) before returning, so a
  caller downstream never observes a half-finished fan-out.
- Cancellation is cooperative: a long-running loop or CPU-bound block
  inside a coroutine should call `ensureActive()`/check
  `coroutineContext.isActive`, or use a cancellable suspending call
  (`delay`, `yield`), so cancelling the parent scope actually stops it
  instead of running to completion regardless.
- Switch dispatcher with `withContext(Dispatchers.IO)` around a blocking
  call (network client without its own suspend API, disk I/O), not by
  launching a whole new coroutine on that dispatcher — `withContext`
  keeps the call sequential and propagates cancellation and the result
  back to the caller.

## Compose: recomposition correctness

- A composable's parameters should be stable (a primitive, a `data class`
  of stable members, an immutable collection, or a type the Compose
  compiler can otherwise infer as stable) — an unstable parameter type
  (a plain `List`/`MutableList`, a mutable var-holding class with no
  `@Stable`/`@Immutable` annotation) defeats the compiler's skip check and
  forces recomposition on every parent recomposition even when the value
  did not change.
- Hoist state to the nearest common ancestor that needs it (a caller, or
  the owning `ViewModel`) instead of trapping it inside a leaf composable
  when a sibling or the caller also needs to read or drive it; a leaf
  composable with no external need for its state can still own it locally
  via `remember`.
- Use `remember { ... }` for state that should survive recomposition but
  not process death or configuration change (a derived value, an
  `Animatable`, a coroutine-backed helper); use `rememberSaveable { ... }`
  when the value must survive activity recreation (rotation, process
  death) — typically UI-only state a `ViewModel` does not already own.
- Never call a suspend function, fire a one-off event (navigation,
  `Toast`, analytics), or start a coroutine directly in a composable's
  body — route it through `LaunchedEffect(key1, ...) { }` (re-runs when a
  key changes) or `DisposableEffect(key) { onDispose { ... } }` (needs
  cleanup), never as a bare statement that runs on every recomposition.
- Pass a lambda's identity stably (hoist it, or wrap it with
  `rememberUpdatedState`/`remember` when it closes over a value that
  changes) instead of allocating a fresh lambda inline in a hot list item
  — an unstable lambda parameter defeats `LazyColumn` item-level skipping
  the same way an unstable data parameter does.

## State exposure from a ViewModel

- Expose state from a `ViewModel` as a read-only `StateFlow`/`SharedFlow`
  backed by a private mutable holder — `private val _state =
  MutableStateFlow(...)` with `val state = _state.asStateFlow()` — never
  the mutable type itself, so only the `ViewModel` can drive state
  changes and the UI layer can only observe.
- Prefer `StateFlow` for "current value" UI state (a screen's latest
  render state, always has a value) and `SharedFlow`/`Channel` for one-off
  events (a snackbar, a navigation trigger) that should not replay a
  stale value to a newly-subscribed collector.
- `LiveData` is legacy for new Compose/coroutine-first code: prefer
  `StateFlow`/`SharedFlow` exposed from the `ViewModel` and collected with
  `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()` in Compose. Keep `LiveData` only where a
  project has already standardized on it or where an unconverted
  `View`-based screen still consumes it.
- Collect a `ViewModel`'s flow in Compose with
  `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()`, not a bare `collectAsState()` — the
  lifecycle-aware variant stops collecting while the screen is stopped
  instead of continuing to observe (and potentially update state) behind
  a backgrounded UI.
